Retained young 1; full-root live 2; remembered source 0; freed candidate 2; inspected old fields 1; validation fields 1.
\image(figures/generation_first.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Only young object 1 is popped by the minor traversal. The printed old-field and validation counts expose additional work rather than equating one young visit with total cost.)
\alttext(A rooted old source 0 points to retained young 1; isolated young 2 is a free candidate. The actual event table includes old-field discovery, remembering, visiting, promotion proposal and freeing proposal.)

> Restrict traversal to young objects, while retaining all boundary targets

The input graph has dense ids 0,…,N−1, ordered root slots and ordered reference
fields. Binary ages classify every object as young 0 or old 1. Let Y be the young
set, R the graph roots and B the targets of all old-to-young fields. The retained
young set is the restricted-graph closure

$$
M=\operatorname{Reach}_{G[Y]}\bigl((R\cap Y)\cup B\bigr),
\qquad B=\{v\in Y:\exists u\notin Y,\ (u,v)\in E\}.
$$
\alttext(Retain the young objects reachable using only young-to-young edges from either direct young roots or targets of any old-to-young reference.)

All seeds are marked before the LIFO stack is processed. Marking on push prevents
cycles, shared targets and duplicate fields from adding duplicate stack entries.
Targets that are old stop the young traversal. Returned young survivor and garbage
ids are ascending; legacy promoted flags are dense over all N ids.

[Ungar’s 1984 Generation Scavenging paper](https://doi.org/10.1145/800020.808261),
Section 8.1, printed 161, describes remembering old objects that reference new ones
as a side effect of stores, then traversing new space from those sources. Its
[original algorithm](https://prl.khoury.northeastern.edu/img/u-sde-1984.pdf)
uses copying, survivor spaces and later tenuring. This report’s fixed-age graph
model omits those runtime policies. It also treats the empirical tendency for
objects to die young as workload-dependent; these constructed heaps do not measure
real object lifetimes. No pause-time or production-runtime claim follows here.

Two native interfaces have deliberately different costs. minor_gc rebuilds boundary
sources on every call and computes a full-root comparison. GenerationalHeap validates
and indexes once, maintains exact reference slots when existing fields change,
and returns a sparse young-only result from collect. Neither collection call
changes object ages, deletes objects or invokes language finalizers. The legacy
names promoted and young_freed describe proposals; they do not apply those changes.

> Reproduce the original seven-object heap

The rooted old chain 0→1→2→3 ends at a field into young 5. Young 4 is a second,
direct root; young 6 is isolated garbage. The generator below reproduces exactly
the original graph and ages. Its native trace supplies both the young marks and
the full comparison. The remembered source is 3, even though the young traversal
never pops it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/generation_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/generational.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const auto input=dm::algorithms::generational_chain_example(4,1,7);const auto t=dm::algorithms::minor_gc_trace(input.graph,input.generations,0);const auto legacy=dm::algorithms::minor_gc(input.graph,input.generations);
 std::print("| object | age | full-root reachable | retained young | free candidate |\n| ---: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n");
 for(int i=0;i<input.graph.num_objects;++i)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,input.generations[i]?"old":"young",bool(t.run.full_reachable[i]),bool(t.run.result.promoted[i]),std::find(t.run.result.young_freed.begin(),t.run.result.young_freed.end(),i)!=t.run.result.young_freed.end());
 const auto&w=t.run.work;std::print("\nYoung visits {}; full visits {}; remembered source {}; old fields inspected {}; full fields inspected {}; validation references {}; dense promotion slots {}; legacy result identical {}.\n",legacy.minor_scanned,legacy.full_scanned,legacy.remembered_set.at(0),w.old_fields,w.full_fields,w.validation.references,w.dense_result_slots,legacy.promoted==t.run.result.promoted&&legacy.young_freed==t.run.result.young_freed);
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(66,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle old,young,dead;old.width=2.6;old.height=1.9;old.font_size=21;old.fill={241,245,249};young=old;young.fill={219,234,254};dead=old;dead.fill={255,237,213};
 for(int i=0;i<4;++i){d.node(std::to_string(i),{double(i)*3.6,0},std::format("OLD {}\n{}",i,i==0?"root":"chain"),old);if(i)d.edge(std::to_string(i-1),std::to_string(i));}
 d.node("4",{0,-3.2},"YOUNG 4\ndirect root",young).node("5",{10.8,-3.2},"YOUNG 5\nold boundary",young).node("6",{5.4,-3.2},"YOUNG 6\nfree candidate",dead)
  .edge("3","5","remembered")
  .text({5.4,2.3},"Original heap: retain young 4 and 5; propose freeing young 6",24).save_svg("build/generation_original.svg");
 if(legacy.minor_scanned!=2||legacy.full_scanned!=6||legacy.remembered_set!=std::vector<int>{3}||legacy.young_freed!=std::vector<int>{6})throw std::runtime_error("original seven-object output");
}
